      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Terraform for dbt Cloud\nThe dbt Cloud provider lets\nTerraform manage the objects of a\ndbt Cloud account: projects,\nconnections, credentials,\nenvironments, environment\nvariables and jobs. dbt Labs\nmaintains it under the source\naddress `dbt-labs/dbtcloud`\n([provider docs](https://registry.\nterraform.io/providers/dbt-labs/db\ntcloud/latest/docs)). It started\nas the community provider\n`GtheSheep/dbt-cloud` ([README](ht\ntps://github.com/dbt-labs/terrafor\nm-provider-dbtcloud/blob/v1.13.1/R\nEADME.md#L5-L7)). The provider\ncalls the dbt Cloud API with a\ntoken. The [Terraform\nmap](../terraform.md) explains\nTerraform itself, and the [dbt map\n](../../dbt/dbt-deep-mental-model.\nmd) explains dbt.\n\nThe unit is one dbt Cloud account.\nThe provider block names the\naccount ID, the API host and the\ntoken ([provider configuration](ht\ntps://github.com/dbt-labs/terrafor\nm-provider-dbtcloud/blob/v1.13.1/d\nocs/index.md#L44-L54)). Your dbt\nmodels live in Git. The dbt Cloud\nobjects say where, how and when\nthose models run. Terraform\nmanages these run settings, not\nthe models.\n\nThe objects sit at two levels\n([Account and Project](#account-an\nd-project-account-and-project)):\n\n- The account holds objects that\nmany projects share, such as\nglobal connections to a data\nplatform, service tokens and user\ngroups.\n- A project holds a Git\nrepository, credentials,\nenvironments, environment\nvariables and jobs.\n\nInside a project, two objects do\nmost of the work:\n\n- An environment decides where and\nhow the code runs: the connection,\nthe credential, the dbt version\nand the Git branch ([The\nEnvironment Decides Where](#the-en\nvironment-decides-where-the-enviro\nnment-decides-where)).\n- A job decides what runs and\nwhen: the dbt commands, the\ntriggers and the target name. Each\njob belongs to one environment.\n\nThree more ideas explain most\nplans. The type of an environment\nswitches dbt features on and off\n([Environment Types Are Switches](\n#environment-types-are-switches-en\nvironment-types-are-switches)).\nThe provider reads most settings\nback from dbt Cloud, but never\nsecrets ([What the Provider Reads \nBack](#what-the-provider-reads-bac\nk-what-the-provider-reads-back)).\nAnd provider versions 0.x and 1.x\nhave different schemas, so the\ndocs must match the pinned version\n([Two Provider Generations](#two-p\nrovider-generations-two-provider-g\nenerations)).\n\nNow compare Snowflake. A dbt Cloud\ncredential names a Snowflake user,\nrole, database and warehouse as\nplain text. Other Terraform, or a\nperson, creates those Snowflake\nobjects. The [Snowflake map](../sn\nowflake/terraform-snowflake.md)\ncovers them.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental\nModels explains the ideas above.\nReading a dbt Cloud Configuration\nsays what you will see and where\nto look first. Pitfalls lists the\nfailures that experienced users\nplan around. Good and Bad\nDecisions compares the main design\nchoices.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-b489306ab05d",
      "originalText": "Terraform for dbt Cloud\nThe dbt Cloud provider lets Terraform manage the objects of a dbt Cloud account: projects, connections, credentials, environments, environment variables and jobs. dbt Labs maintains it under the source address `dbt-labs/dbtcloud` ([provider docs](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/dbt-labs/dbtcloud/latest/docs)). It started as the community provider `GtheSheep/dbt-cloud` ([README](https://github.com/dbt-labs/terraform-provider-dbtcloud/blob/v1.13.1/README.md#L5-L7)). The provider calls the dbt Cloud API with a token. The [Terraform map](../terraform.md) explains Terraform itself, and the [dbt map](../../dbt/dbt-deep-mental-model.md) explains dbt.\n\nThe unit is one dbt Cloud account. The provider block names the account ID, the API host and the token ([provider configuration](https://github.com/dbt-labs/terraform-provider-dbtcloud/blob/v1.13.1/docs/index.md#L44-L54)). Your dbt models live in Git. The dbt Cloud objects say where, how and when those models run. Terraform manages these run settings, not the models.\n\nThe objects sit at two levels ([Account and Project](#account-and-project-account-and-project)):\n\n- The account holds objects that many projects share, such as global connections to a data platform, service tokens and user groups.\n- A project holds a Git repository, credentials, environments, environment variables and jobs.\n\nInside a project, two objects do most of the work:\n\n- An environment decides where and how the code runs: the connection, the credential, the dbt version and the Git branch ([The Environment Decides Where](#the-environment-decides-where-the-environment-decides-where)).\n- A job decides what runs and when: the dbt commands, the triggers and the target name. Each job belongs to one environment.\n\nThree more ideas explain most plans. The type of an environment switches dbt features on and off ([Environment Types Are Switches](#environment-types-are-switches-environment-types-are-switches)). The provider reads most settings back from dbt Cloud, but never secrets ([What the Provider Reads Back](#what-the-provider-reads-back-what-the-provider-reads-back)). And provider versions 0.x and 1.x have different schemas, so the docs must match the pinned version ([Two Provider Generations](#two-provider-generations-two-provider-generations)).\n\nNow compare Snowflake. A dbt Cloud credential names a Snowflake user, role, database and warehouse as plain text. Other Terraform, or a person, creates those Snowflake objects. The [Snowflake map](../snowflake/terraform-snowflake.md) covers them.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental Models explains the ideas above. Reading a dbt Cloud Configuration says what you will see and where to look first. Pitfalls lists the failures that experienced users plan around. Good and Bad Decisions compares the main design choices.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "terraform-dbt-cloud"
      }
    },
